# Every operation that names a position in a process notation. One row per filed
# `pm:Operation/foreignId`: the notation, the id inside it, and the operation here that claims it.
#
# A reference out of the document
#   Like an `asrt:Part` and an `asrt:Elimination/between`, this points outside the document. The
#   part has composition/part_references.sqlc and a rule that checks it resolves; the elimination
#   has eliminations/references.sqlc and an observation, because the schema allows a `between` that
#   lands nowhere. References of one type owe the same treatment, and a gap between them shows in a
#   directory listing without reading any SQL.
#
# No rule may judge it
#   A field no rule reads has nothing holding it in place, and this one can have no rule.
#   `pm:ForeignId`'s own annotation rules the check out: a notation plus an id names a position in
#   one file that no authority publishes, so a receiver sent looking for `Task_17` in a normative
#   list finds nothing there and reads it as a defect in the list. There is nothing to resolve
#   against, so checks/unresolved_part.sqlc's shape copied here would accuse every filing. It gets
#   the answer `between` got: report, never assert.
#
# The other arm is a fact, not a silence
#   `notationPosition` is required and takes `pm:StatedForeignId`, so an operation that names no
#   position says why: `none`, it is in no notation and somebody looked; `unmeasured`, a notation
#   exists and nobody located this operation in it; `notApplicable`, this filing has no notation at
#   all. This relation is the stated arm. queries/observations/17-notation-references.sqlc reports
#   both, because the whole population is the finding and the stated arm alone would hide it.
#
# The notation is a language, not a document
#   That is the difference from a part. A part names another filing, and `filing_identity` resolves
#   it. This names the OMG BPMN model URI, and the id names a node in whichever BPMN document
#   travels beside the filing. So the pair names a position in a document nobody here holds, which
#   is exactly why crossing to it costs the receiver nothing.
-- pm:Operation/pm:notationPosition, the stated arm: a notation plus an id.
SELECT o.filing, o.label, o.foreign_notation, o.foreign_id
FROM ( :compose(entries/operations.sqlc) ) o
WHERE o.foreign_notation IS NOT NULL
